Z||000044||Phil||12-28-2000||12:03 PM||||If 29 frogs catch 29 flies in 29 minutes, how many frogs are required to catch 87 flies in 87 minutes?||||reg||1
Z||000045||fropuff||12-28-2000||12:20 PM||||I'd have to say 87.  If there were 29 frogs, they all ate 1 fly per minute, equalling 29 flies in 29 minutes.  When there are 87 flies and 87 minutes, 87 frogs should eat one per minute.  <BR>Or: <P>     29(FL)xM=29<BR>     29/29=1 (FL/M)<BR>     29FL/MxFR=29M<BR>     29/29=1  (FR/M)<P>Then:<P>     87(FL)xM=87 <BR>     87/87=1  (FL/M)<BR>     87FL/MxFR=87M<BR>     87/87=1  (FR/M)<P>Can you tell I'm in Algebra?<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000046||StuGood||12-28-2000||12:30 PM||||Stu says it takes 29 frogs.<P>Makes sense - you're giving them 87/29 as much time, to catch 87/29 as many flies - it shouldn't require any more frogs. Obviously, Stu has done <B>way</B> too many manhour estimates.  <P>If you like to do it algebraically, figger the original frog team scarfed 'em at an average rate of:<P>RateAvg = (29 flies / (29 frogs * 29 min))  = (1/29) fly per frog*minute.<P>Now the new goal for team Frog, is 87 flies in 87 minutes, or 1 fly per minute.  How many frogs does that take?  Well...<P>Frogs req'd= (1 fly/min)/(1/29 fly/frog*min)= 29 frogs.  <P>...assuming they're still hungry   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Besides, if you used 87, they'd get all tongue-tied   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000047||Phil||12-28-2000||04:05 PM||||Stu---Good||||reg||1
Z||000048||StuGood||12-29-2000||01:31 PM||||Thanks, Phil.  I have 2 unfair advantages, when it comes to riddles like these: 1) I think like a 3rd-grader, and 2) In the summertime, I've watched toads sit on my basement window ledges scarfing bugs at night.  The toads don't even have to move - the light from the windows attracts the bugs, and the toads just sit there and rake 'em in.<P>I still like your first riddle.  But it was kind of discouraging to think about, "What is greater than God, (and) more evil than the devil...?"  My thought at that moment was, "I don't know, but whatever it is, I sure don't want to meet it..."  Then I came to my senses - nothing is greater than God - and as I said before, it all fell into place.  I felt a lot better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||7
Z||000049||fropuff||12-29-2000||02:05 PM||||Now I see why I'm getting a B in Algebra.  ||||reg||1
Z||000050||StuGood||12-29-2000||02:17 PM||||B's are plenty good.  Think I used to get those in high school, and C's, in algebra.  <P>Besides, you got the "racecar" one.  Shoot, you know those palindromes forwards and backwards, fropuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">! ||||reg||7
